#!/bin/sh
# script/fastpath-footprint: the IPC fastpath must stay small enough to live in L1i.
#
# **Why this exists, and it is the oldest performance argument in microkernels.** Liedtke's
# "On micro-Kernel Construction" (SOSP 1995) argued that Mach's IPC was slow because of the CACHE
# FOOTPRINT of its hot path rather than anything inherent to microkernels: a kernel that touches a
# lot of memory per IPC evicts the *application's* working set, so the cost shows up as capacity
# misses spread through the workload instead of as time in the kernel. L4 was written against that,
# and seL4 keeps a hand-maintained fastpath for the same reason. notes/benchmarks.md has the full
# argument, the frontier cache sizes it is measured against, and the target;
# design/roadmap/0132-the-fastpath-footprint.md is the milestone that owns this gate.
#
# **What this measures**: an UPPER BOUND on the instruction footprint of an IPC round trip, in two
# parts, because they are two different quantities and averaging them would hide both.
#
#   ipc_send_receive   the transitive closure of non-cold calls from the SEND/RECEIVE roots. Whole
#                   symbol sizes, so a cold tail inside a hot function counts. Upper bound.
#   ipc_call_reply  the same closure from the CALL/RECEIVE_CAP/REPLY roots. **This is the shape the
#                   system actually runs**, and it is the larger of the two; see below.
#   ipc_fastpath    the worse of those two, which is what a round trip costs. Derived, and kept
#                   under its old name because the tree cites it in a dozen places.
#   syscall_entry   the trap entry, the exception dispatcher, the return path and
#                   `syscall::dispatch`, FLAT with no closure. A syscall traverses one path through
#                   the decoder, but the decoder's own bytes are on every syscall, and its other
#                   arms are not on this path at all.
#
# **Two closures and not one, since 2026-09-04 (milestone 188 phase 1).** This gate measured only
# the SEND/RECEIVE shape until then, which is the shape of the `ipc_rtt_el0` benchmark and of
# essentially no service in this tree: a client issues `CALL` and a server issues `RECEIVE_CAP` then
# `REPLY`, and `kernel/src/bench/ipc.rs`'s own `call_reply` doc calls that "the one-endpoint shape
# real services use". Measured on the same binary, the CALL/reply closure is **29% larger** on
# aarch64 (7,576 against 5,888), because `ipc_receive_cap` and `ipc_call` carry the one-shot Reply
# mint, the capability-table insert and the `WaitRole::Reply` parking that DECISIONS §12 (Call/Reply
# IPC: a one-shot reply capability) requires. Averaging them would hide both, and reporting only the
# smaller one understated the number this gate exists to bound. `ipc_fastpath` is the max rather
# than the sum because one round trip is one shape or the other, never both.
#
# Names: `ipc_send_receive` and `ipc_call_reply` are **provisional** (milestone 188's lane, 2026-09-04).
# Every name in this tree is an architect's; these two are shipped so the measurement can exist and
# are
# expected to change.
#
# **`syscall_entry` is not like for like across the three ISAs, and the reason is architectural.**
# aarch64 and riscv64 route a syscall through the same vector table and cause decoder as every other
# exception, so their entry numbers carry the whole trap path. x86_64 does not: `syscall` jumps
# through `IA32_LSTAR` and never touches the 256-entry IDT, so its entry number covers four symbols
# and legitimately excludes 3,330 bytes of stubs and trap handlers that the other two ISAs' figures
# have no equivalent of. x86_64's entry figure being the smallest of the three is that fact, not a
# leaner decoder. The per-symbol reasoning is at `ENTRY` below. `ipc_fastpath` IS comparable across
# all three: it closes over portable Rust and the symbol sets agree.
#
# **What it reports against, since 2026-09-21: distance, not drift.** Every figure is printed as
# bytes, as a multiple of the **4 KiB target**, and as a share of the **32 KB L1i** that binds
# (radon's SiFive U74, the smallest among the machines this tree runs on). Those two constants are
# the reason this gate exists and they were not in its output: it said `+1.4% against baseline
# (7028)`, which measures movement away from whatever the binary happened to be last month and means
# something different after every compiler change. Drift is still what FAILS, because a 5% jump
# inside one pull request is a mistake somebody just made; the budget is reported and not gated,
# for the reason set out at `L1I` below. See `notes/benchmarks/kernel-footprint-and-caches.md`, "The
# target".
#
# **What it is not.** It is not a cache measurement. No cache is modelled by icount and the HVF host
# has an L1i several times larger than the boards, so nothing in this tree can yet observe the
# effect this bounds. Milestone 74's PMU counters on milestone 127's Jetson TX1 are what turn this
# from arithmetic into measurement. Until then this is a tripwire on the quantity, not the harm.
#
# BUGS
#   **This measured two of the three architectures until 2026-08-27**, with nothing in the script
#   recording the omission, which is the same gap `script/stack-frame-check` had and the
#   architecture-list sweep found. x86_64 is measured now. Anything added here that is per-architecture
#   (`ENTRY`, the `case` below, `CALL`) has to be extended when a fourth ISA arrives, and the
#   `ENTRY` set is a judgement about that ISA's trap architecture rather than a string edit.
#
#   **The bound is drift from a recorded figure, not distance from a target**, so headroom is
#   whatever the last save happened to leave and a branch can be near the bound without having
#   done anything. On 2026-09-21 riscv64 sat at +2.2% on `ipc_send_receive` and +4.7% on
#   `syscall_entry` before the branch under test contributed anything, so a 150-byte change failed
#   the gate while a much larger one would have passed a year earlier. Milestone 541 (a timed
#   window that excludes preemption) hit this and did not re-save; a lane is re-shaping this gate
#   to report distance from a 4 KiB target and a 32 KB L1i instead.
#
#   **A field added to `cpu::PerCpu` moves these numbers even when the fastpath never reads it**,
#   which is the failure mode this script is worst at explaining. `PERCPU[id]` indexes an array of
#   that struct, so its size decides whether the address is a shift or a multiply: taking it from
#   128 to 136 bytes cost 150 bytes here on riscv64 and 38 on `syscall_entry`, with the code that
#   wrote the new field costing nothing measurable. `kernel/src/cpu.rs` now asserts the size is a
#   power of two so that arrives as a failing build instead of as this gate's output.
#
# Usage:
#   script/fastpath-footprint            # measure all three ISAs and check against the baselines
#   script/fastpath-footprint --save --why "<reason>"
#                                         # re-record the baselines (a statement that a change is
#                                         # intended). --why is required and repeatable, as for
#                                         # script/bench --save (milestone 302 (a
#                                         # baseline records what it was saved against)).
#   script/fastpath-footprint --arch aarch64
#   script/fastpath-footprint --features fastpath_pad  # E3 (milestone 134): build with an extra
#                                         # kernel feature (comma-separated for more than one) and
#                                         # measure THAT binary. Never implies --save: a padded
#                                         # build's numbers are an experiment's reading, not a new
#                                         # baseline, so they print and are never checked or written.
#   NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD=0 NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT=1640 \
#     script/fastpath-footprint --arch riscv64 --features board,bench,single_hart,fastpath_pad --layout
#                                         # milestone 134's layout control: also print a hash of the
#                                         # hot path's instructions with addresses normalised away
#                                         # (equal across every E3 image, which is the proof they run
#                                         # the same code), where each hot symbol landed, its L1i set
#                                         # on riscv64, and where the data sections start. The two
#                                         # variables size the pad and the shift (kernel/build.rs).
#                                         # Changes nothing without the flag.
#
# Name: provisional. `fastpath` is the field's own word for the thing measured: it is what seL4
# calls its hand-written common-case IPC path, so a reader who knows the literature meets a term
# they already have. `footprint` is the quantity rather than the verdict, which is deliberate. The
# known inconsistency, stated rather than left for a reader to notice: `-check` is this
# directory's suffix for a gate that fails a build (`interleaving-check`, `qemu-check`,
# `stack-frame-check`), and by that convention this should be `fastpath-footprint-check`. It is
# not, because the name would then be nine syllables to say a thing an architect may want spelled
# differently anyway, and that is an argument from typing cost rather than from design. DECISIONS
# §134's split points the other way and would put a reporter in the noun half, where this already
# sits. Nothing depends on it. no architect has ratified it.
#
# See notes/benchmarks.md and script/stack-frame-check, whose shape this copies: derive a number
# from the built kernel, compare it against a rule, fail the build. That gate exists because
# `sched::reap_region_objects` grew a 6816-byte frame that compiled without a warning and was found
# only when CI faulted one run in five. A hot-path footprint has the same shape: it grows silently.
#
# BUGS
#
#   - **`arches` below is a hardcoded string and derives nothing.** All three supported
#     architectures are in it today, so nothing is silently unmeasured, but a fourth ISA arriving
#     will be measured here only when somebody remembers to type it. That is finding 11 of
#     notes/architecture-list-sweep.md and milestone 186 owns the mechanism; taking a hand-fix here
#     would be a second hand-fix of the same defect. (The older text of this bug said the script
#     gated two of three and that x86_64 had no baseline. Both were true when written and neither
#     is now: x86_64 was widened in on 2026-08-27, `bench/fastpath-x86_64.txt` exists, and
#     `kernel/src/arch/x86_64/fastpath_pad.rs` does not, so `--features fastpath_pad --arch x86_64`
#     is still the one thing here that only works on two of three.)
#   - **The footprint baselines name their nightly only from the next `--save`.** Symbol sizes move
#     with the compiler for the same reason icount counts do, so since 2026-09-24 `--save` writes
#     the `# toolchain:`, `# date:` and `# why:` lines `bench/baseline-*.txt` carries (milestone
#     302's shape; see `SAVE` below for why there is no `# qemu:` line). The three committed files
#     predate that and were deliberately not re-saved to get it: calef's 2026-09-21 ruling is that
#     they are not to be re-saved while what a byte of footprint buys cannot be measured (see `L1I`
#     below, and milestone 370 (a layout control)). Until that save, a failure here cannot say which
#     compiler its floor came from, and the check says so. The erosion is not hypothetical: measured
#     2026-09-21 on `nightly-2026-09-20`, every figure on every ISA was above its baseline, and
#     riscv64's `syscall_entry` was **+4.7% against a 5% band**.
#   - **Once stamped, nothing compares the stamp against the pin.** `script/lint`'s
#     baseline-toolchain check globs `bench/baseline-*.txt` only. Widening it to these files would
#     make every toolchain bump re-save the footprint floors, which is the re-save the 2026-09-21
#     ruling holds back, so that is an architect's call rather than a lint edit. Until then the
#     stamp is
#     a record a failing run prints, not a gate.
#   - **`syscall_entry` is flat, so an inlining decision can move bytes into it that no syscall
#     fetches.** Three instances in two days on 2026-09-04, each closed with `#[inline(never)]` on
#     the folded callee. `design/roadmap/0368-a-flat-entry-set-counts-bytes-no-syscall-fetches.md`
#     holds them and the options. Milestone 188 phase 2 fixed the largest *structural* instance of
#     the same shape (aarch64's sixteen-entry vector table, at `ENTRY_SLICE`) but did nothing about
#     the inlining one, which is a different mechanism with the same symptom.
#   - **The pin check (milestone 796 (pin the hot trap path's placement)) costs two more release
#     builds of aarch64 and riscv64 on every default run**, the shift pair, and proves less on x86_64,
#     which has no `fastpath_pad` and gets only the containment half. Its hot set is this script's
#     closure, so it inherits the closure's blind spots: a callee reached only through a conditional
#     branch or a function pointer is on the path, outside the section, and not reported.
#   - **`ipc_call_reply` is still an upper bound and a loose one**, for the reason the whole gate is:
#     whole symbol sizes, so a cold tail inside `ipc_call` or `ipc_receive_cap` counts. Milestone 188
#     phase 3 outlined the cold tails it could find; the rest are inside branches this script cannot
#     see, because its `CALL` regex deliberately does not follow conditional branches.

set -e
# A heavy job: wait for one of the machine's heavy-job slots first (helpers/heavy_slot.py).
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh"
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

save=""
# Newline-separated, because POSIX sh has no arrays and a reason is prose that may contain spaces.
why=""
arches="aarch64 riscv64 x86_64"
features=""
layout=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        --save) save=1 ;;
        --why) shift; [ -n "$(printf '%s' "$1" | tr -d '[:space:]')" ] && why="$why$1
" ;;
        --why=*) v="${1#--why=}"; [ -n "$(printf '%s' "$v" | tr -d '[:space:]')" ] && why="$why$v
" ;;
        --arch) shift; arches="$1" ;;
        --features) shift; features="$1" ;;
        --layout) layout=1 ;;
        *) echo "script/fastpath-footprint: unknown argument $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    shift
done

# **`--features` never gates and never saves.** A padded build's footprint is an experiment's
# reading (E3, design/roadmap/0134-the-measurements-that-decide.md), not a candidate baseline: it is
# expected to fail a 5% tolerance built for the un-padded kernel, and checking it would just print
# the failure the experiment is deliberately causing. `--save` with `--features` would silently
# replace the real baseline with an experimental one, which is the mistake this refuses outright.
if [ -n "$features" ] && [ -n "$save" ]; then
    echo "script/fastpath-footprint: --save with --features would overwrite the baseline with an experimental build; not doing that" >&2
    exit 2
fi

# **A save records why, beside the numbers**, the rule milestone 302 made for the icount floors and
# for the same reason: a commit message is read once, on the day it is written, and a floor that
# cannot say why it holds its value cannot tell the next reader drift from a regression. Refused
# here rather than at the write so it costs a second instead of three release builds. A blank
# `--why` is dropped above, so `--why ""` does not satisfy it.
if [ -n "$save" ] && [ -z "$why" ]; then
    echo "script/fastpath-footprint: --save needs --why \"<reason>\"; the reason is written into the" >&2
    echo "  baseline beside the numbers. Repeat --why once per reason when one save covers several moves." >&2
    exit 2
fi

tools="$(rustc --print sysroot)/lib/rustlib/$(rustc -vV | grep '^host:' | cut -d' ' -f2)/bin"
if [ ! -x "$tools/llvm-objdump" ]; then
    echo "script/fastpath-footprint: llvm-objdump not found; run script/bootstrap (llvm-tools)." >&2
    exit 1
fi

# One release build of $arch for $target with $features, measured and checked. A function because
# the pin check below measures two more builds of the same ISA and must read them the same way.
measure() {
    # **The RELEASE binary, and that is the whole point.** A debug kernel's `.text` is about twice
    # the size (notes/benchmarks.md measured 348 KiB against 168 KiB on aarch64), so a debug
    # footprint would be measuring the absence of the optimizer rather than the shape of the path.
    if [ -n "$features" ]; then
        echo "==> $arch: building the release kernel (--features $features)"
        cargo build -p kernel --release --target "$target" --features "$features" >/dev/null
    else
        echo "==> $arch: building the release kernel"
        cargo build -p kernel --release --target "$target" >/dev/null
    fi

    rc=0
    tmp=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$tmp"' EXIT
    "$tools/llvm-objdump" -d --no-show-raw-insn "target/$target/release/kernel" > "$tmp/dis.txt"

    # **The workspace's own `#[cold]` claims, read out of the source** (milestone 188 phase 3).
    # See `COLD` below for why this is derived rather than listed.
    cold_fns=$(grep -rhA3 --include='*.rs' '#\[cold\]' kernel/src crates \
        | grep -oE 'fn [a-z_0-9]+' | sed 's/fn //' | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')

    ARCH="$arch" DIS="$tmp/dis.txt" BASE="bench/fastpath-$arch.txt" SAVE="$save" WHY="$why" \
        COLD_FNS="$cold_fns" NOCHECK="$features" LAYOUT="$layout" PIN_DUMP="${pin_dump:-}" \
        NM="$tools/llvm-nm" ELF="target/$target/release/kernel" \
        LAYOUT_OUT="target/fastpath-layout-$arch${features:+-$(echo "$features" | tr ',' '+')}-pad${NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD:-1}-shift${NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT:-0}.txt" python3 - <<'PYEOF' || rc=1
import os, re, sys, collections, datetime

arch, dis_path, base_path, save = os.environ["ARCH"], os.environ["DIS"], os.environ["BASE"], os.environ["SAVE"]


def pinned_nightly():
    """The channel `rust-toolchain.toml` pins, read the way `xtask/src/bench.rs`'s `pinned_nightly`
    reads it, so the two headers cannot disagree about what the pin is."""
    try:
        for line in open("rust-toolchain.toml"):
            if line.lstrip().startswith("channel"):
                m = re.search(r'"([^"]+)"', line)
                return m.group(1) if m else None
    except OSError:
        pass
    return None


def stamped_toolchain(path):
    """The `# toolchain:` a baseline was saved under, or None for a file that predates the stamp."""
    for line in open(path):
        m = re.match(r"^#\s*toolchain:\s*(\S+)\s*$", line)
        if m:
            return m.group(1)
    return None

# **Local labels are not symbols.** The riscv build emits 1,994 `.Lpcrel_hi*` headers, which would
# otherwise chop real functions into fragments and make every size wrong. Skipping them keeps the
# enclosing function accumulating, which is what a footprint wants.
SYMHDR = re.compile(r"^([0-9a-f]+) <(.+)>:$")
# aarch64 calls with `bl`; riscv64 with `jalr` (and `jal`); x86_64 with `callq`. All three
# tail-call with their unconditional branch (`b` / `j` / `jmp`). Every form objdump annotates with a
# target symbol is followed, because a tail call is still a fetch of the callee's bytes.
#
# **Conditional branches are deliberately not here, on any of the three.** `b.ne`, `bne` and `jne`
# all fail the `\s` after the mnemonic, which was an accident of the original two-ISA pattern and is
# now a checked choice: every conditional branch in the x86_64 kernel's five root symbols targets an
# offset inside the branching symbol itself (`<ipc_send+0x2b8>`), which the `t != cur` test below
# would discard anyway. Counting them would add no edge and would risk following a cold arm.
CALL = re.compile(r"\b(bl|blr|jal|jalr|b|j|callq?|jmpq?)\s+[^<]*<([^>+]+)")

# **A linker-script boundary is not a function, but objdump may print it as one.** Milestone 796
# (pin the hot trap path's placement) put `__text_hot_start`, a global symbol, at the same address
# as the first hot function, and when that function is local llvm-objdump heads the block with the
# global marker instead. On x86_64 that hid `sched::current_cap` and took 5% off `ipc_send_receive`
# before this mapping existed. So a header named like a linker-script bound (`__<x>_start`,
# `__<x>_end`) is renamed to the function `llvm-nm` says starts there, and the bounds themselves are
# kept for the pin check below.
import subprocess
MARKER = re.compile(r"^__\w+_(start|end)$")
func_at, marks = {}, {}
for ln in subprocess.run([os.environ["NM"], os.environ["ELF"]], capture_output=True,
                         text=True).stdout.splitlines():
    f = ln.split()
    if len(f) != 3:
        continue
    if MARKER.match(f[2]):
        marks[f[2]] = int(f[0], 16)
    elif f[1] in "tT" and not f[2].startswith(".L"):   # riscv64's `.Lpcrel_hi*` are labels
        func_at.setdefault(int(f[0], 16), f[2])


def fn_name(name):
    """A symbol objdump printed, with a linker-script bound replaced by the function it shares an
    address with. Call targets need it as much as headers do: a call to the first hot function is
    annotated with the bound's name."""
    return func_at.get(marks.get(name), name) if MARKER.match(name) else name


def header_name(m):
    name = m.group(2)
    return func_at.get(int(m.group(1), 16), name) if MARKER.match(name) else name


start, last, calls = {}, {}, collections.defaultdict(set)
cur = None
for line in open(dis_path, encoding="utf8", errors="replace"):
    line = line.rstrip("\n")
    m = SYMHDR.match(line)
    if m:
        name = header_name(m)
        if name.startswith(".L"):
            continue          # a local label inside the current function, not a new one
        cur = name
        start.setdefault(cur, int(m.group(1), 16))
        continue
    if cur is None:
        continue
    a = re.match(r"^([0-9a-f]+):", line)
    if a:
        last[cur] = int(a.group(1), 16)
    c = CALL.search(line)
    if c:
        t = fn_name(c.group(2))
        if not t.startswith(".L") and t != cur:
            calls[cur].add(t)

# The last instruction's address plus its width. 4 on aarch64; riscv64 mixes 2- and 4-byte
# instructions, so 4 is the conservative choice, which is the right direction for an upper bound.
#
# **x86_64 is the case where 4 is a guess in both directions**, since its instructions are 1 to 15
# bytes, and it was worth measuring rather than asserting. Summing the exact sizes instead (the gap
# to the next symbol header, padding included) gives 6,619 bytes against this formula's 6,639 for the
# closure and 1,632 against 1,637 for the entry set: a 0.3% over-count. Two effects nearly cancel.
# LLVM parks `int3` padding after most x86 functions and objdump lists those bytes as instructions,
# so `last` is usually already the final padding byte and the +4 lands past the symbol; against that,
# a function ending in a 5-byte tail `jmp` is under-counted by one. 0.3% is a fifteenth of the
# tolerance below, so this is noise rather than a bound that needs a different formula.
size = {s: last.get(s, start[s]) - start[s] + 4 for s in start}

# **The roots: what "the IPC fastpath" is.** These lists are a decision, not a heuristic, and they
# are the question notes/benchmarks.md named as the thing blocking this gate from existing. Matched
# by regex because mangled names carry a per-target crate disambiguator that is not stable.
#
# `schedule`, `finish_switch` and `current_cap` are in both, because both shapes reach them: the
# capability lookup that IS the security check, and the general scheduler re-selection that a
# hand-written fastpath would be trying to skip.
#
# **Each root names its own module**, because a mangled name follows the defining module, not the
# `pub use` that keeps the Rust path: `ipc_*` are `kernel5sched10rendezvous...`
# (kernel/src/sched/rendezvous.rs), `current_cap` is `kernel5sched12capabilities...`
# (capabilities.rs), and `schedule` and `finish_switch` are `kernel5sched6switch...` (switch.rs).
# Each entry below is `<len><module><len><function>` as v0 mangling writes it, and the shape's
# regex and the guard below are both built from that one list.
#
# `schedule` and `finish_switch` are roots even though every IPC root reaches them today, because
# the closure follows only unconditional direct calls and tail branches. If a root came to reach
# them through a conditional branch or a function pointer, the walk would lose their bytes while
# the IPC still ran them. Naming them keeps their bytes in the closure whatever call shape reaches
# them.
#
# **Every root must match a symbol**, checked below, or the run fails. `INLINED` names the two that
# are `#[inline(always)]` badge-0 wrappers (see `ipc_call_badged` below) and so have no symbol in a
# release build; they stay in the lists so a build that does emit them still measures them.
SHAPES = [
    ("ipc_send_receive",
     ["10rendezvous8ipc_send", "10rendezvous15ipc_send_badged", "10rendezvous11ipc_receive",
      "6switch8schedule", "6switch13finish_switch", "12capabilities11current_cap"]),
    ("ipc_call_reply",
     ["10rendezvous8ipc_call", "10rendezvous15ipc_call_badged", "10rendezvous15ipc_receive_cap",
      "10rendezvous9ipc_reply", "6switch8schedule", "6switch13finish_switch",
      "12capabilities11current_cap"]),
]
INLINED = {"10rendezvous8ipc_send", "10rendezvous8ipc_call"}
# **`ipc_call_badged` is a root because it IS the CALL body** (milestone 599 (a frame per filesystem
# client channel), 2026-09-26). The syscall's `CALL` dispatches to it, and `ipc_call` became an
# `#[inline(always)]` wrapper that passes badge 0, so the `ipc_call` symbol no longer exists in a
# release build. Without this root the closure silently lost the whole CALL body and reported an
# 18-30% "shrink" on every ISA; a root list that does not follow a rename measures less than the
# path the system runs, which is the failure this gate exists to rule out.
#
# **`ipc_send_badged` is a root for the same reason, one shape over** (milestone 613 (a system log
# service: the in-memory half), 2026-10-03). A plain `SEND` now carries its badge, so the syscall
# dispatches to `ipc_send_badged` and `ipc_send` is the inlined badge-0 wrapper. The first CI run
# without this root reported `ipc_send_receive` shrinking 16-18% on all three ISAs: the same rename
# blindness, caught by the same 5% band.

# **Cold, so excluded from the closure.** Two families, for two different reasons.
#
# Panic and formatting: reached only when the kernel is already dying, so those bytes are never
# fetched on a healthy IPC. LLVM parks them at the end of a function, on their own cache lines.
#
# Teardown: `finish_switch` reaps a *finished* thread, which drags in `KernelStack::drop`,
# `untyped::destroy`, `revoke_region`, `delete_frame_caps` and the unmap path. That branch runs when
# a thread exits and never during an IPC. Including it put 11.2 KiB on this number when the honest
# figure is 5.6, which is exactly the over-count that would make the gate meaningless.
#
# **The second family is DERIVED from the source, not listed here** (milestone 188 phase 3), and
# that is the more important half. This regex is a hand-maintained list in a gate, which is rung two
# at best: a lane that wanted a smaller number could edit it, and a lane that outlined a genuinely
# cold arm got no credit for it unless it remembered to. Both happened. Phase 3 outlined four cold
# arms out of `finish_switch`, `schedule` and the four stale-rendezvous returns, and the closure got
# **332 bytes BIGGER on aarch64**, because the walk simply followed the new calls and counted the
# same bytes under new names.
#
# So the claim lives where a reader and the optimizer both already meet it, `#[cold]` on the
# function, and the shell above greps the workspace for it. A lane still cannot shrink this number
# by editing the gate; it can only shrink it by writing a `#[cold]` in the source, which is a claim
# in a diff that a reviewer sees and that LLVM acts on. `strand_callers_of` and its two siblings
# used to be named in the regex (milestone 254, 2026-09-04) and are now covered by their own
# attributes; left in the closure they put 1,363 bytes on x86_64's figure, 20%.
#
# **What is NOT derivable, and stays a regex.** The panic and formatting family is libcore's and
# carries no attribute we can read. The teardown callees (`revoke`, `destroy`, `unmap`,
# `KernelStack`, `AddressSpace`, `drop_in_place`, `delete_frame_caps`) are reached only through
# `finish_switch`'s reap arm and are ordinary functions on their own paths, so `#[cold]` would be a
# lie about them; they are excluded here because of who reaches them, which is a property of this
# walk rather than of the function. Including them put 11.2 KiB on a 5.6 KiB figure.
COLD = re.compile(
    r"panic|expect_failed|unwrap_failed|slice_index|9panicking|4core3fmt|assert"
    r"|6revoke|7destroy|drop_in_place|delete_frame_caps|unmap|KernelStack|12AddressSpace"
    + "".join(
        # Length-prefixed, because both Rust manglings write `<len><ident>` and a bare name would
        # match any symbol containing it as a substring.
        r"|\d+" + re.escape(f) + r"\b"
        for f in sorted(os.environ.get("COLD_FNS", "").split())
    )
)

# **The entry set, and x86_64 is not the same shape as the other two.** On aarch64 and riscv64 a
# syscall IS an exception: `svc` lands in the same vector table as a page fault, `ecall` in the same
# `stvec` handler as a timer interrupt, so the whole vector and the whole cause decoder are bytes
# every syscall fetches and both lists carry them. On x86_64 a ring-3 `syscall` reads `IA32_LSTAR`
# and jumps, consulting **no IDT entry at all** (kernel/src/arch/x86_64/trap.s says so at its
# `x86_syscall_entry` header: "THIS IS NOT AN IDT VECTOR AND THE DIFFERENCES ALL MATTER"). So the
# correct x86_64 set is NARROWER than the other two, not wider, and a reader comparing the three
# numbers should not read the difference as this port having a leaner decoder.
#
# Included, each because a `syscall` fetches its bytes:
#   x86_syscall_entry    the `IA32_LSTAR` target, written by `init_syscall` in exceptions.rs. The
#                        hardware jumps straight here.
#   x86_syscall_handler  "the one Rust function every `syscall` reaches" (exceptions.rs), which
#                        bumps the counter and tail-calls the portable dispatcher.
#   isr_restore          the return path. `x86_syscall_entry` ends `jmp isr_restore`, and trap.s
#                        names it "the RISC-V twin of `trap_return`", which riscv64's list already
#                        carries for the same reason. Shared with the IDT path; a syscall runs it.
#   syscall::dispatch    as on both other architectures.
#
# Excluded, each because a `syscall` never reaches it:
#   isr_0 .. isr_255     the 256 IDT stubs, 2,412 bytes of them. Reached only through an IDT gate.
#   isr_common           the stubs' shared save path, which `x86_syscall_entry` duplicates inline
#                        rather than jumping to (it has a different frame to reconstruct).
#   x86_trap_dispatch,   the Rust trap handlers. These ARE the twins of riscv64's
#   x86_trap_body        `riscv_trap_dispatch` / `riscv_trap_body`, which riscv64's list includes,
#                        and that is exactly the asymmetry: on riscv64 the syscall arrives through
#                        them, on x86_64 it does not. Including them here for symmetry with the
#                        other list would put 918 bytes on this number that no syscall fetches.
#   user_return,         first entry to ring 3, and the interrupt-stack trampoline. Once per thread
#   dispatch_on_...      and per IDT trap respectively, neither per syscall.
#
# **aarch64 counts ONE vector entry, not the whole table** (milestone 188 phase 2, 2026-09-04).
# `exception_vectors` is 2,020 bytes and is not code in the ordinary sense: vectors.s builds it as
# sixteen `VECTOR_ENTRY` macros at `.balign 0x80`, because "the hardware requires 2048-byte
# alignment. 16 entries x 128 bytes = 2048." Each entry is self-contained (SAVE_CONTEXT inline, then
# `bl exception_dispatch`, then `b exception_restore`), so an `svc` from EL0 lands in exactly one of
# them, index 8 (lower EL AArch64 synchronous), and fetches **at most 128 bytes**. The other 1,892
# are page faults, IRQs, kernel-mode traps and the AArch32 entries this kernel will never support.
#
# 128 and not the ~104 bytes of instructions the macro actually emits, because the entry occupies an
# aligned 128-byte slot and the fetch is of cache lines rather than of instructions. This stays an
# upper bound, which is the direction every other choice in this script leans.
#
# **This is an accounting fix and nothing got faster.** aarch64's `syscall_entry` drops about 1,892
# bytes on the day this lands. The reason it is a fix rather than a saving is that the old figure was
# gameable in exactly the way AGENTS.md's ranking function forbids: a lane could have "improved" this
# gate by 57% without changing one fetched instruction.
#
# riscv64 needs no equivalent. `stvec` is in direct mode (exceptions.rs: "all traps to one handler"),
# so there is one `trap_entry` and no table to over-count. x86_64 needs none either, for the reason
# already argued below: it consults no IDT entry at all.
ENTRY_SLICE = {"exception_vectors": 128}

# `exception_restore` runs on the return leg of every syscall on aarch64 and was in neither figure
# until 2026-09-04. It is the twin of riscv64's `trap_return` and x86_64's `isr_restore`, both of
# which their lists already carry, so its absence was an asymmetry rather than a judgement.
ENTRY = {
    "aarch64": [r"^exception_vectors$", r"^exception_dispatch$", r"^exception_restore$",
                r"kernel7syscall8dispatch"],
    "riscv64": [r"^trap_entry$", r"^trap_return$", r"^riscv_trap_dispatch$", r"^riscv_trap_body$",
                r"kernel7syscall8dispatch"],
    "x86_64": [r"^x86_syscall_entry$", r"^x86_syscall_handler$", r"^isr_restore$",
               r"kernel7syscall8dispatch"],
}[arch]

shape_totals, shape_syms = {}, {}
for label, names in SHAPES:
    # Each root on its own, so one that stops matching (a module split, a rename) fails the run
    # instead of shrinking the closure inside the 5% band.
    lost = [n for n in names
            if n not in INLINED and not any(re.search("kernel5sched" + n, s) for s in size)]
    if lost:
        print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: {label} root(s) {', '.join(lost)} matched no symbol; "
              "the module or the name moved", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    rootre = re.compile("kernel5sched(" + "|".join(names) + ")")
    roots = [s for s in size if rootre.search(s)]
    seen, stack = set(), list(roots)
    while stack:
        s = stack.pop()
        if s in seen or s not in size or COLD.search(s):
            continue
        seen.add(s)
        stack.extend(calls.get(s, ()))
    shape_totals[label] = sum(size[s] for s in seen)
    shape_syms[label] = seen

closure_total = max(shape_totals.values())

entry_syms = [s for s in size if any(re.search(p, s) for p in ENTRY)]
entry_total = sum(ENTRY_SLICE.get(s, size[s]) for s in entry_syms)
total = closure_total + entry_total

# **The two numbers this gate is actually about, and until 2026-09-21 it printed neither.**
#
# This script's own first line says the fastpath must stay small enough to live in L1i. That is a
# physical budget, and what the output reported was `+1.4% against baseline (7028)`: drift from
# whatever the binary happened to be last month. The facts a reader needs were in
# notes/benchmarks.md and nowhere near the gate, so the gate's number meant something different
# after every compiler change and said nothing about the constraint it exists to serve.
#
#   L1I     32 KB, radon's SiFive U74 (notes/benchmarks.md's cache table names it the binding
#           constraint: the smallest L1i among the machines this tree actually runs on, and it sits
#           at the bottom of the frontier range anyway, so it tracks the board list rather than a
#           number somebody liked).
#   TARGET  4 KiB, about an eighth of that L1i. notes/benchmarks/kernel-footprint-and-caches.md's
#   "The target" derives the
#           fraction from Liedtke's argument rather than from roundness: the constraint is not that
#           the kernel fits, it is that the kernel leaves most of L1 intact for the application,
#           because cache pollution is what Mach actually charged its users.
#
# **Neither is gated, and that is a decision rather than an omission** (calef, 2026-09-21). The
# tree is 1.5x to 2x over the target, so a gate on it would fail on the day it was written and be
# turned off on the next. Shrinking the fastpath to make it pass would be optimising against a
# target whose value nobody here can currently measure: milestone 370 (a layout control) exists
# precisely because the perturbation experiments that would price a byte of footprint cannot yet
# tell footprint from addresses. Whether 4 KiB is still the right number belongs with
# milestone 132 (the fast path's footprint) and milestone 188 (the IPC fastpath), and waits on 370.
#
# So: distance is REPORTED, because it is the quantity, and it means the same thing after a
# compiler change. Drift against the committed baseline stays the thing that FAILS, because a 5%
# jump inside one pull request is a mistake somebody just made, which is what a gate catches well.
L1I = 32 * 1024
TARGET = 4 * 1024


def against(n):
    """`n` as a share of the L1i that binds, and as a multiple of the target."""
    return f"{n / TARGET:5.2f}x target  {100 * n / L1I:4.1f}% of L1i"


print(f"    budget: {TARGET} B target, {L1I} B L1i (radon's SiFive U74, the smallest we run on)")
for label, _ in SHAPES:
    tail = "  <- the shape the system runs" if label == "ipc_call_reply" else ""
    print(f"    {label:15s}{shape_totals[label]:6d} B  {against(shape_totals[label])}"
          f"  over {len(shape_syms[label])} symbols{tail}")
print(f"    ipc_fastpath   {closure_total:6d} B  {against(closure_total)}"
      f"  the worse of the two shapes")
# No target ratio on the entry set: the 4 KiB target is stated over the IPC fastpath's
# instructions, and the trap path is not part of what it bounds. Its L1i share is still the
# quantity that matters, since a syscall fetches these bytes too.
print(f"    syscall_entry  {entry_total:6d} B  {' ' * 15}{100 * entry_total / L1I:4.1f}% of L1i"
      f"  over {len(entry_syms)} symbols (flat, no closure)")
print(f"    total          {total:6d} B  {against(total)}"
      f"  ({total/1024:.2f} KiB), an upper bound")
# The third distance, and the only one the tree has already voted on. §144 (a delta and a ceiling)
# decides an absolute 16 KiB per architecture on exactly this `total`, derived in
# notes/target-hardware.md as half of the 32 KB L1i, which is where Liedtke's argument genuinely
# weakens. It is DECIDED and not built: no check here enforces it, so printing the fraction is what
# keeps the decision visible against the number it was made about.
print(f"    {100 * total / (16 * 1024):.0f}% of the 16 KiB ceiling §144 (a delta and a ceiling) decides and does not yet enforce")

HOT_WAY = 16 * 1024
hot = (set().union(*shape_syms.values()) | set(entry_syms)) - {"fastpath_pad_body"}

def norm_name(n):
    n = re.sub(r"\.llvm\.\d+", "", re.sub(r"Cs[0-9A-Za-z]+_", "", n))
    # `.Lpcrel_hi388` renumbers, and aarch64's `adr` names constants `anon.<cgu hash>.N`,
    # whose hash follows the codegen unit and so the feature set.
    n = re.sub(r"^anon\.[0-9a-f]+\.", "anon.", n)
    return re.sub(r"^(\.L[A-Za-z_]+?)\d+", r"\1", n)

def short(n):
    n = norm_name(n)
    if not n.startswith("_R"):
        return n
    toks, i = [], 0
    while i < len(n):
        m = re.match(r"(\d+)", n[i:])
        if m:
            k = int(m.group(1)); i += len(m.group(1))
            toks.append(n[i:i + k]); i += k
        else:
            i += 1
    toks = [t for t in toks if re.match(r"^[A-Za-z_]\w*$", t)]
    return "::".join(toks[-2:]) if toks else n


# **The pin: every hot symbol inside `.text.hot`, and that section on a way boundary** (milestone
# 796, 2026-10-07). The linker scripts gather every `.text.hot.*` input section, sorted by name, into
# one block on a 16 KiB boundary that nothing but the boot stub precedes, so its addresses do not
# move when code is added anywhere else. That holds only for the functions that are in it. A new
# function on the path, a rename, or an inlining change that gives the path an out-of-line callee
# leaves a hot symbol in ordinary `.text`, where it moves with every unrelated change again. This
# names it and fails. The fix is a `#[link_section = ".text.hot.<name>"]` on the function (or a
# `.section` directive in the assembly), which is what every member already carries.
#
# The other direction is reported and not gated: a symbol in the section that this walk did not
# reach costs its bytes inside the block and nothing more. Two are there by design (x86_64's
# `isr_common`, which falls through into `isr_restore`; aarch64's vector table, of which a syscall
# runs one 128-byte slot); anything else is a stale attribute worth removing.
pin_bad = False
pin_dump = []
# An experiment's build (--features) reports an escape as a reading rather than a failure. E3's own
# `maybe_pad` is on its path and carries a `.text.hot` section like the rest.
pin_out = sys.stdout if os.environ.get("NOCHECK") else sys.stderr
lo, hi = marks.get("__text_hot_start"), marks.get("__text_hot_end")
if lo is None or hi is None:
    print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: no __text_hot_start/__text_hot_end; the linker script lost "
          f"its hot section (milestone 796)", file=pin_out)
    pin_bad = True
else:
    escaped = sorted((s for s in hot if not lo <= start[s] < hi), key=lambda x: start[x])
    extra = sorted(short(n) for a, n in func_at.items() if lo <= a < hi and n not in hot)
    print(f"    pin: .text.hot {hi - lo} B at {lo:#x}, {lo % HOT_WAY} B into a 16 KiB way; "
          f"{len(hot) - len(escaped)} of {len(hot)} hot symbols inside")
    if lo % HOT_WAY:
        print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: .text.hot starts {lo % HOT_WAY} B past a 16 KiB "
              f"boundary; the linker script's ALIGN is gone", file=pin_out)
        pin_bad = True
    for s in escaped:
        print(f"{'    pin: ' if pin_out is sys.stdout else 'fastpath-footprint: '}{arch}: "
              f"{short(s)} is on the hot path but outside .text.hot "
              f"(at {start[s]:#x}); give it a `.text.hot.*` section", file=pin_out)
        pin_bad = True
    if extra:
        print(f"    pin: also in .text.hot, off the measured path: {', '.join(extra)}")
    pin_dump = [f"{short(s)} {start[s]:#x} {size[s]}"
                for s in sorted(hot, key=lambda x: start[x]) if lo <= start[s] < hi]
    pin_dump.insert(0, f".text.hot {lo:#x} {hi:#x}")
# Read by the shell's shift check below: the section's bounds and each member's address and size.
if os.environ.get("PIN_DUMP"):
    with open(os.environ["PIN_DUMP"], "w") as f:
        f.write("\n".join(pin_dump) + "\n")
if pin_bad and os.environ.get("NOCHECK"):
    pin_bad = False             # an experiment's build reports the escape and does not gate on it

# **`--layout`: where the hot path landed, and proof that nothing else moved** (milestone 134's
# layout control, 2026-09-19; notes/footprint-perturbation.md). E3 compares kernels that differ in
# where code sits, so each image needs two facts before it is worth a boot: that it executes the
# same instructions as its siblings, and where its hot symbols fall in radon's L1i.
#
# The first is a hash of the instruction text of every hot symbol (both closures plus the entry
# set, minus E3's own sled), with **address operands normalised and nothing else**. The classes
# are exactly the ones a diff between two pad sizes turned up, and the count of lines each one
# touched is printed so the normalisation is visible rather than trusted: a direct call or branch
# target (kept as a function-relative offset, or as the callee's name), an `auipc`/`adrp` page or
# upper immediate, and the low-12 immediate that pairs with one (tracked by register). A real
# constant, a register, a mnemonic or an instruction count cannot hide in those classes.
#
# The second is per-symbol addresses and, on riscv64, set indices for the U74's L1i: 32 KiB, 2-way,
# 64-byte lines, virtually indexed (SiFive U74-MC Core Complex Manual 21G3.02.00, §4.2.2), so 256
# sets indexed by VA bits 6 to 13. `mod8` is there because §4.2.6 of the same manual says the BTB
# predicts a taken branch or jump without a bubble only when the target is 8-byte aligned. Other
# ISAs print addresses without sets: no L1i geometry here was read off a manual for them.
if os.environ.get("LAYOUT"):
    import hashlib
    starts = sorted(start.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1])
    by_addr = {}
    for s, a in start.items():
        by_addr.setdefault(a, s)

    # One pass over the disassembly, keeping only hot symbols' instructions.
    text, cur = collections.defaultdict(list), None
    for line in open(dis_path, encoding="utf8", errors="replace"):
        line = line.rstrip("\n")
        m = SYMHDR.match(line)
        if m:
            if not m.group(2).startswith(".L"):
                cur = header_name(m)
            continue
        a = re.match(r"^([0-9a-f]+):\s*(.*)$", line)
        if a and cur in hot:
            text[cur].append((int(a.group(1), 16), a.group(2)))

    counts = collections.Counter()
    TARGET = re.compile(r"0x([0-9a-f]+) <([^>]+)>")
    UPPER = re.compile(r"^(auipc|adrp)\s+(\w+),")
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    out = open(os.environ["LAYOUT_OUT"], "w")
    ninsn = 0
    for s in sorted(hot, key=norm_name):
        lo, hi = start[s], start[s] + size[s]
        # Registers holding an `auipc`/`adrp` result. One stays in the set until an instruction
        # redefines it, because LLVM keeps such a base live and loads through it several times
        # (`riscv_trap_body` does, through `s3`); "consumed on first use" missed exactly that.
        pending = set()
        for addr, ins in text.get(s, []):
            ninsn += 1
            ins = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", ins.strip())
            m = UPPER.match(ins)
            if m:
                pending.add(m.group(2))
                ins = f"{m.group(1)} {m.group(2)}, UPPER"
                counts["upper"] += 1
                h.update((ins + "\n").encode())
                out.write(ins + "\n")
                continue
            # A low-12 of zero prints as an alias, so fold the two that occur back into the form
            # the non-zero case takes: `mv a1, a1` is `addi a1, a1, 0`, `jalr ra` is `jalr 0(ra)`,
            # and aarch64's `mov x8, x8` is `add x8, x8, #0`.
            al = re.match(r"^(mv|mov) (\w+), (\w+)$", ins)
            if al and al.group(3) in pending:
                ins = ("addi" if al.group(1) == "mv" else "add") + f" {al.group(2)}, {al.group(3)}, 0x0"
            al = re.match(r"^jalr (\w+)( <.*)?$", ins)
            if al and al.group(1) in pending:
                ins = f"jalr 0x0({al.group(1)})" + (al.group(2) or "")
            t = TARGET.search(ins)
            if t:
                tgt = int(t.group(1), 16)
                if lo <= tgt < hi:
                    rep = f"<@+{tgt - lo:#x}>"
                else:
                    rep = "<" + norm_name(fn_name(re.sub(r"\+0x[0-9a-f]+$", "", t.group(2)))) + ">"
                # `jalr 0x52(ra) <callee>` after `auipc ra`: the offset is the pair's low half.
                ins = re.sub(r"-?0x[0-9a-f]+(\(\w+\))", r"LO\1", ins[:t.start()]) + rep
                counts["target"] += 1
            for r in sorted(pending):
                # `addi a1, a1, -0x3a`, `ld a0, -0x3b4(s3)`, `add x8, x8, #0x10`,
                # `ldr x0, [x8, #0x20]`: the low-12 immediate that pairs with an upper.
                pat = (rf"(-?0x[0-9a-f]+|-?\d+)\({r}\)|(\b{r}, )(#?-?0x[0-9a-f]+|#?-?\d+)$"
                       rf"|(\[{r}, )#-?0x[0-9a-f]+\]")
                new, n = re.subn(pat, lambda mm: (f"LO({r})" if mm.group(1) is not None
                                 else (mm.group(2) + "LO") if mm.group(2)
                                 else mm.group(4) + "LO]"), ins)
                if n:
                    ins = new
                    counts["lo12"] += n
            # A write to a tracked register ends its tracking (after its own immediate was
            # normalised above, so `addi a0, a0, LO` counts and then a0 is an ordinary pointer).
            # The destination is the first operand except for stores and branches, which have none.
            mn, _, ops = ins.partition(" ")
            if not re.match(r"^(s[bhwd]|fs[wd]|sc\.|st|b|j|c?bn?z|tbn?z|ret|cmp|tst)", mn):
                dest = ops.split(",")[0].strip()
                pending.discard(dest)
            # Any annotation left (a call through `jalr LO(ra) <callee>`) names its symbol the
            # normalised way: crate disambiguators and LLVM's hash suffixes change with features.
            ins = re.sub(r"<([^>@][^>]*)>", lambda mm: "<" + norm_name(mm.group(1)) + ">", ins)
            h.update((ins + "\n").encode())
            out.write(ins + "\n")

    out.close()
    print(f"    layout: normalised text in {os.environ['LAYOUT_OUT']}")
    print(f"    layout: code hash {h.hexdigest()[:16]} over {ninsn} instructions in {len(hot)} "
          f"hot symbols (sled excluded; normalised {counts['target']} targets, "
          f"{counts['upper']} upper, {counts['lo12']} low-12)")
    if "fastpath_pad" in os.environ.get("NOCHECK", "").split(","):
        print(f"    layout: NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD={os.environ.get('NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD', '1 (unset)')} "
              f"NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT={os.environ.get('NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT', '0 (unset)')}")
    # The sections after `.text` are page-aligned, so they move in whole pages when `.text` crosses
    # a boundary. That is a layout channel of its own: radon's L1d is 32 KiB 4-way, an 8 KiB way,
    # so an odd number of pages moves every static to a different set (U74-MC manual §4.4.1).
    sect = {k: v for k, v in marks.items()
            if k in ("__text_end", "__data_start", "__bss_start", "__image_end")}
    if sect:
        print("    layout: " + "  ".join(f"{k} {v:#x}" for k, v in sorted(sect.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1])))
    pad = [s for s in start if s == "fastpath_pad_body"]
    blk = [s for s in start if s == "fastpath_layout_shift"]
    if pad:
        print(f"    layout: fastpath_pad_body at {start[pad[0]]:#x}, {size[pad[0]]} bytes"
              + (f"; fastpath_layout_shift at {start[blk[0]]:#x}" if blk else ""))
        # **How much of the hot path this pad actually moves.** Only the symbols after the sled
        # move when it grows. The linker scripts place it after `.text.hot`, so with milestone 796's
        # pin this reads 0 of N, and an E3 image perturbs no hot instruction
        # (notes/footprint-perturbation/step-0-under-the-pin.md).
        moved = [s for s in hot if start[s] > start[pad[0]]]
        share = 100 * sum(size[s] for s in moved) / max(1, sum(size[s] for s in hot))
        print(f"    layout: the sled precedes {len(moved)} of {len(hot)} hot symbols, "
              f"{share:.0f}% of their bytes; only those move when it grows")
    SETS = {"riscv64": 256}.get(arch)
    for s in sorted(hot, key=lambda x: start[x]):
        a = start[s]
        where = f"set {(a >> 6) % SETS:3d}  " if SETS else ""
        print(f"    layout: {a:#x}  {where}line+{a & 63:2d}  mod8 {a & 7}  {short(s)}")
    lines = set()
    for s in hot:
        lines.update(range(start[s] >> 6, (start[s] + size[s] - 1 >> 6) + 1))
    pages = {ln >> 6 for ln in lines}
    aligned = sum(1 for s in hot if start[s] % 8 == 0)
    summary = (f"    layout: {len(lines)} lines over {len(pages)} pages; "
               f"{aligned} of {len(hot)} hot symbols start 8-byte aligned")
    if SETS:
        per = collections.Counter(ln % SETS for ln in lines)
        over = sum(1 for n in per.values() if n > 2)
        excess = sum(n - 2 for n in per.values() if n > 2)
        summary += f"; {len(per)} of {SETS} L1i sets hold hot lines, {over} hold more than 2 ({excess} lines past the ways)"
    print(summary)

# **What is checked.** The two shapes and the entry set. `ipc_fastpath` is recorded and printed but
# not checked on its own, because it is the max of two numbers that are each already checked: a
# separate check on it could only ever fire alongside one of theirs and would say less about which.
GATED = tuple(
    [(label, shape_totals[label]) for label, _ in SHAPES] + [("syscall_entry", entry_total)]
)

nocheck = os.environ.get("NOCHECK", "")
if nocheck:
    # E3 (milestone 134): a padded build reports and never gates, and it reads against the real
    # baseline for a human rather than pretending it has none. The comparison is informational
    # only; `sys.exit(0)` regardless of the ratio, because failing here would just be
    # `fastpath-footprint`'s own 5% tripwire firing on a build that was deliberately made to trip
    # it, which tells nobody anything they did not already know from asking for `--features`.
    if os.path.exists(base_path):
        want = {}
        for line in open(base_path):
            line = line.strip()
            if line and not line.startswith("#"):
                k, v = line.split()
                want[k] = int(v)
        for key, got in GATED + (("ipc_fastpath", closure_total),):
            ref = want.get(key)
            if ref:
                print(f"    {key}: {got/ref:.2f}x the un-padded baseline ({ref} -> {got})")
    else:
        print(f"    (no baseline at {base_path} to compare against)")
    sys.exit(0)

# **The provenance lines, in the shape `bench/baseline-*.txt` carries** (milestone 302): one fact per
# line, first, so `script/lint`'s `^#\s*toolchain:\s*(\S+)\s*$` would read this file unchanged if it
# is ever widened to it. The pin rather than the running compiler, for `xtask/src/bench.rs`'s
# reason: rustup resolves the compiler from the pin, so they are one fact, and a one-shell override
# is not a fact about the repository. **There is no `# qemu:` line, deliberately.** Nothing here
# runs an emulator: these numbers are a function of the compiler that emitted the bytes and of the
# `llvm-objdump` that read them, and that objdump is the pinned toolchain's own `llvm-tools`
# (`tools` above), so `# toolchain:` names both. A `# qemu:` line would record an instrument that
# did not take part.
if save:
    stamp = "# toolchain: %s\n# date: %s\n" % (
        pinned_nightly() or "unrecorded",
        datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%d"))
    stamp += "".join("# why: %s\n" % w.strip() for w in os.environ["WHY"].splitlines() if w.strip())
    with open(base_path, "w") as f:
        f.write(stamp)
        f.write(
            "# bench/fastpath-%s.txt: an upper bound on the IPC fastpath's instruction footprint.\n"
            "# Written by script/fastpath-footprint --save. Updating this file is a statement that a\n"
            "# footprint change is intended and understood; do it in the commit that causes it.\n"
            "# See notes/benchmarks.md for what the numbers mean and what they are measured against.\n"
            "#\n"
            "# `ipc_send_receive` and `ipc_call_reply` are the two IPC shapes, measured separately since\n"
            "# milestone 188 phase 1 because the system runs the second and this gate measured only the\n"
            "# first. `ipc_fastpath` is DERIVED, the worse of the two, kept under its old name because\n"
            "# the tree cites it; it is not checked separately. Both shapes and `syscall_entry` are.\n"
            "ipc_send_receive %d\nipc_call_reply %d\nipc_fastpath %d\nsyscall_entry %d\n"
            % (arch, shape_totals["ipc_send_receive"], shape_totals["ipc_call_reply"],
               closure_total, entry_total)
        )
    print(f"    wrote {base_path}")
    sys.exit(1 if pin_bad else 0)

if not os.path.exists(base_path):
    print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: no baseline at {base_path}; run --save", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)

want = {}
for line in open(base_path):
    line = line.strip()
    if line and not line.startswith("#"):
        k, v = line.split()
        want[k] = int(v)

# **Five percent, tighter than the icount tripwire's ten**, and the reason is that these numbers are
# static. icount drifts across builds because the compiler remakes inlining decisions for unrelated
# reasons (notes/benchmarks.md records that measurement); a symbol size moves when the code moves.
# A looser bound here would let real creep through.
#
# **Symmetric on purpose** (milestone 156). A one-sided `delta > TOL` only ever catches growth, so
# a real improvement (an extraction, a smaller data structure) can land and sit unclaimed as slack
# in the baseline forever, and a stale-loose baseline silently widens the tripwire's real tolerance:
# if the code shrinks 20% and nobody re-saves, a later regression of +15% against the *current* code
# is actually +15% against a number 20% too generous, and the gate still says "within bound." A
# shrink needs no justification for *why* (unlike growth, which must say what caused it), only an
# explicit `--save` that locks in the tighter number, so the baseline never drifts stale by more
# than one tolerance band in either direction. Measured run-to-run variance on an un-padded build is
# 0% (three back-to-back runs with no code change produced byte-identical symbol sizes, since a
# release build has no incremental-compilation or codegen-unit nondeterminism to speak of), so TOL
# is not narrower than anything the data supports in either direction.
TOL = 0.05
bad = pin_bad
for key, got in GATED:
    ref = want.get(key)
    if ref is None:
        print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: baseline has no {key}; run --save", file=sys.stderr)
        bad = True
        continue
    delta = (got - ref) / ref if ref else 0.0
    if delta > TOL:
        print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: {key} grew {delta*100:.1f}% ({ref} -> {got}), over the {TOL*100:.0f}% bound.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print("  Shrink it, or re-record with --save --why \"...\" in the commit that causes the growth.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        bad = True
    elif delta < -TOL:
        print(f"fastpath-footprint: {arch}: {key} shrank {-delta*100:.1f}% ({ref} -> {got}), over the {TOL*100:.0f}% bound.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        print("  The baseline is stale: re-record with --save --why \"...\" to lock in the tighter bound (a shrink needs no defence, only a reason on record).",
              file=sys.stderr)
        bad = True
    elif abs(delta) > 0.001:
        # Labelled `drift` since 2026-09-21, because that is what it is: movement against a number
        # this tree happened to record on some earlier day, on some earlier compiler. The lines
        # above are the distance to the budget, and they are what the gate is about; this is the
        # secondary signal that catches a sudden jump inside one pull request.
        print(f"    drift: {key} {delta*100:+.1f}% against baseline ({ref}), within the {TOL*100:.0f}% band")

# **Which compiler the floor came from, said at the moment it matters.** A toolchain bump moves
# symbol sizes with no commit responsible, so a failure against a floor saved under another nightly
# is not evidence against this branch. This does not fail anything on its own: the pin is checked
# nowhere for these files (the BUGS entry above says why), and an unstamped file is the truthful
# state of every floor saved before 2026-09-24.
if bad:
    was, pin = stamped_toolchain(base_path), pinned_nightly()
    if was is None:
        print(f"  {base_path} does not say which nightly produced it, so a compiler bump since the "
              f"last save cannot be ruled out. The next --save records it.", file=sys.stderr)
    elif pin and was != pin:
        print(f"  {base_path} was saved under {was}; this build is {pin}. Part or all of the "
              f"movement may be the compiler's, not this branch's.", file=sys.stderr)
sys.exit(1 if bad else 0)
PYEOF
    rm -rf "$tmp"
    trap - EXIT
    return "$rc"
}

status=0
# shellcheck disable=SC2086  # $arches is a deliberate space-separated list; POSIX sh has no arrays
for arch in $arches; do
    case "$arch" in
        aarch64) target=aarch64-unknown-none-softfloat ;;
        riscv64) target=riscv64imac-unknown-none-elf ;;
        # No RUSTFLAGS is set anywhere in this script, which is what makes this arm a one-liner:
        # `x86_64-unknown-none` needs `-C relocation-model=static` (a kernel cannot be a static-PIE)
        # and gets it from `.cargo/config.toml`. A bare `RUSTFLAGS` in the environment REPLACES that
        # table rather than appending to it, and rust-lld then fails with `relocation R_X86_64_32
        # cannot be used against local symbol`. Found by the `script/stack-frame-check` lane that
        # widened it the same week; if this script ever grows a RUSTFLAGS of its own, re-add the
        # flag explicitly.
        x86_64) target=x86_64-unknown-none ;;
        *) echo "script/fastpath-footprint: no target for arch $arch" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac

    measure || status=1

    # **The pin under a shift: the hot section's addresses with the rest of `.text` moved** (milestone
    # 796). The check inside `measure` proves every hot symbol is in `.text.hot`; this proves the
    # section does not move when unrelated code is added, by building two kernels that differ only
    # in milestone 370's `NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT` (0, and 20,520 bytes of unreferenced zeros) and
    # comparing each hot symbol's address and size. 20,520 is a 16 KiB way, a page and 40 bytes, so
    # every later line, set and page moves, the data sections included. **It must exceed 16 KiB**:
    # with 4,136 a linker script that put the sled AHEAD of the hot section still passed, because the
    # section's 16 KiB ALIGN absorbed the whole displacement (found 2026-10-07 by breaking the order
    # on purpose). x86_64 has no `fastpath_pad` (BUGS above), so it gets only the containment check.
    # Not run with --features or --save, which measure one build on purpose.
    case "$arch" in aarch64|riscv64)
        if [ -z "$features" ] && [ -z "$save" ] && [ "$status" = 0 ]; then
            echo "==> $arch: the hot section under a 20,520-byte text shift (two fastpath_pad builds)"
            pin_tmp=$(mktemp -d)
            features=fastpath_pad
            export NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD=0
            for shift in 0 20520; do
                export NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT="$shift"
                pin_dump="$pin_tmp/$shift.txt"
                measure >"$pin_tmp/$shift.log" 2>&1 || {
                    cat "$pin_tmp/$shift.log" >&2
                    echo "fastpath-footprint: $arch: the shift-$shift build failed" >&2
                    status=1
                }
            done
            features="" pin_dump=""
            unset NIFE_FASTPATH_PAD NIFE_FASTPATH_SHIFT
            if [ "$status" = 0 ] && cmp -s "$pin_tmp/0.txt" "$pin_tmp/20520.txt"; then
                echo "    pin: $(($(wc -l < "$pin_tmp/0.txt") - 1)) hot symbols at the same address and size with the rest of .text shifted 20,520 B"
            elif [ "$status" = 0 ]; then
                echo "fastpath-footprint: $arch: the hot section moved when only unrelated code did:" >&2
                diff "$pin_tmp/0.txt" "$pin_tmp/20520.txt" >&2 || true
                status=1
            fi
            rm -rf "$pin_tmp"
        fi ;;
    esac
done

[ "$status" = 0 ] && [ -z "$save" ] && [ -z "$features" ] && echo "fastpath-footprint: within the drift band on every ISA checked; the distance to the 4 KiB target is above, and is reported rather than gated"
[ "$status" = 0 ] && [ -n "$features" ] && echo "fastpath-footprint: measured (--features $features; not checked against the baseline)"
exit "$status"
